<Post title="Holding the line" part={3} date="2026-09-17" series="rshunterbtt">
	<p class="tagline">
		If the device will hold a connection and narrate its own state, the bridge should stop
		behaving like a taxi and start behaving like a phone call. This is the rewrite: one
		connection held open, authenticated, subscribed — and supervised by a ladder of
		recovery actions for when the radio inevitably gives up.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the shape of it</h2>
	<p>
		The new bridge inverts the old design. Instead of connecting for every operation, it
		opens one connection and keeps it. Everything else — status reads, battery polls,
		commands — happens <em>through</em> that connection, and the connection is rebuilt from
		scratch the moment it drops.
	</p>
	<Mermaid>{`sequenceDiagram
		participant B as bridge (Rust)
		participant T as tap timer
		Note over B,T: connection setup (must be re-run in full after any drop)
		B->>T: scan + connect
		B->>T: discover services
		B->>T: write 4-byte password  (ff81)
		B->>T: request connection interval (raw HCI)
		B->>T: subscribe to notifications (CCCD on ff82)
		Note over B,T: steady state — connection held open indefinitely
		loop state changes
			T-->>B: ff82 notification (zone state)
		end
		B->>T: on_off command (write ff83/ff86/ff8b)
		T-->>B: status response on MQTT`}</Mermaid>
	<p>
		The critical property is that setup is atomic: password auth and the notification
		subscription <em>do not survive a disconnect</em>. There is no partial-resume. Every
		reconnect re-runs the whole sequence, which is exactly what the probing in part 2 said
		would be necessary.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>talking to the radio directly</h2>
	<p>
		Two parts of setup I couldn't do through the friendly BLE library, because they are
		link-layer operations rather than GATT operations.
	</p>
	<p>
		The first is the connection interval. Setting it in the host's Bluetooth configuration
		is not enough: the controller starts a connection using the peripheral's advertised
		preferred parameters, and those override the host defaults. The only reliable lever is a
		raw <code>LE Connection Update</code> command straight down an HCI socket — so I wrote a
		small module that opens a raw HCI channel, finds the connection handle, and sends the
		update. That module is also where the retry logic lives, because the device's slowness
		means the first request sometimes times out before it lands.
	</p>
	<p>
		The second is diagnostics. The interesting failure modes on these hosts are HCI-level —
		a disconnection reason byte, a command status for the random-address opcode — and no BLE
		library exposes them. So I had the bridge open an HCI <em>monitor</em> channel and decode
		a couple of event types itself. At the time I wrote it this was "diagnostic only", and I
		deliberately kept its escalation decisions out of it. It is also, accidentally, the
		foundation for the entire fix in part 5 — but it shipped with two encoding bugs that
		meant it decoded precisely nothing, and I didn't notice, because it was only ever
		supposed to be logs.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the resilience ladder</h2>
	<p>
		A persistent connection that never comes back is worse than no connection, so the
		supervisor escalates through increasingly aggressive recovery actions, driven purely by
		consecutive failures. It deliberately does <em>not</em> try to diagnose a root cause
		first — it just counts, and acts:
	</p>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>rung</th><th>trigger</th><th>action</th><th>rate limit</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<td>0</td>
				<td>any failure</td>
				<td>reconnect with exponential backoff (1 s → 60 s)</td>
				<td>—</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>1</td>
				<td>5 consecutive failures in 10 min</td>
				<td>power-cycle the BLE adapter over D-Bus</td>
				<td>1 / 15 min</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>2</td>
				<td>10 further failures after rung 1</td>
				<td>reboot the host (raw <code>reboot(2)</code> syscall)</td>
				<td>1 / 30 min, ≤3 / 24 h</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>3</td>
				<td>24 h reboot cap reached</td>
				<td>keep retrying, log loudly, stop escalating</td>
				<td>—</td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		Two details make this work in the field. The adapter power-cycle goes through the
		system bus, not a shelled-out command, so it doesn't care whether the host runs an init
		system that exposes a binary for it. And the host reboot is a direct syscall, because
		these are Alpine/OpenRC machines: there is no login manager and no D-Bus reboot method
		to call. The bridge falls back to the kernel.
	</p>
	<p>
		The subtlety that would be easy to get wrong: <strong>the rate-limit state has to be
		persisted to disk</strong>. A reboot restarts the bridge process — which means an
		in-memory counter cannot rate-limit the very reboot it just performed. The timestamps
		live in a small file that is fsynced before the reboot, and reloaded on startup, so the
		escalation ladder survives its own recovery action.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>building for a 2016 board in 2026</h2>
	<p>
		The targets are Pi Zero W boards — ARMv6, musl, three of them. I cross-compile the
		bridge statically from a normal machine and copy it onto each host as a single binary,
		run under a small service definition that gives it the Bluetooth and system bus access
		it needs. The dependency injection points that make the code testable — the BLE client,
		the MQTT client, the database writer, the resilience controller — are all traits, so I
		can exercise the interesting logic in unit tests with mocks, and only a thin shell
		touches real hardware.
	</p>
	<p>
		There is an obvious irony in this part of the story: a test suite that verifies my model
		of the hardware is only as good as that model. Several tests here encoded the assumption
		that a certain status byte was a boolean — and they passed, happily, while the hardware
		meant something else entirely. More on that shortly.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the state of play after the rewrite</h2>
	<p>
		The bridge now holds its connection, authenticates, subscribes, and reproduces device
		state over MQTT. Paired with the home automation cutover, six irrigation zones become
		ordinary controllable things. Everything was deployed, and it worked — for a while.
	</p>
	<p>
		Then the home automation UI started showing every zone as running, and the timers
		started fighting back about the connection interval. The two bugs that survived a
		rewrite, a test suite, and a deployment are the two remaining parts of this series — and
		between them, they took me longer to find than the rewrite took to write.
	</p>
</Post>
